solution_original:
5. Reference：HKDSE.Math M2 2014 Q12
(a) $|P|=-y-x=-(x+y)$
Since $x y>0, x$ and $y$ are both positive or both negative,
$$
\text { so } x+y \neq 0 \text { and }|P| \neq 0 \text {. }
$$
Therefore,$P$ is invertible.
(b)
$$
\begin{aligned}
P^{-1}= & \frac{-1}{x+y}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
-1 & -1 \\
-x & y
\end{array}\right)=\frac{1}{x+y}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 1 \\
x & -y
\end{array}\right) \\
P^{-1} M P & =\frac{1}{x+y}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 1 \\
x & -y
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3+x & -y \\
-x & 3+y
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{cc}
y & 1 \\
x & -1
\end{array}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{x+y}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3 & 3 \\
x(3+x+y) & -y(3+x+y)
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{cc}
y & 1 \\
x & -1
\end{array}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{x+y}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3(x+y) & 0 \\
0 & (x+y)(3+x+y)
\end{array}\right) \\
& =\underbrace{\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3 & 0 \\
0 & 3+x+y
\end{array}\right)}_{n \text { times }} \\
P^{-1} M^{n} P & =\overbrace{\left(P^{-1} M P\right)\left(P^{-1} M P\right) \ldots\left(P^{-1} M P\right)} \\
& =\left(P^{-1} M P\right)^{n} \\
& =\underbrace{\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3^{n} & 0 \\
0 & (3+x+y)^{n}
\end{array}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
(c) Note that $\left(\begin{array}{cc}3+(-1) & -(-3) \\ -(-1) & 3+(-3)\end{array}\right)$ and $(-1)(-3)=3>0$ .
Putting $x=-1$ and $y=-3$ ,we have
$$
M=\left(\begin{array}{ll}
2 & 3 \\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right) \text { and } P=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
-3 & 1 \\
-1 & -1
\end{array}\right) .
$$
$$
\begin{aligned}
P^{-1}\left(\begin{array}{ll}
2 & 3 \\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right)^{2 n} P & =\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3^{2 n} & 0 \\
0 & (3-1-3)^{2 n}
\end{array}\right) \\
\left(\begin{array}{ll}
2 & 3 \\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right)^{2 n} & =P\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3^{2 n} & 0 \\
0 & (3-1-3)^{2 n}
\end{array}\right) P^{-1} \\
& =\left(\begin{array}{cc}
-3 & 1 \\
-1 & -1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3^{2 n} & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{array}\right)\left(\frac{1}{-4}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 1 \\
-1 & 3
\end{array}\right)\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{4}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
-3^{2 n+1} & 1 \\
-3^{2 n} & -1
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{cc}
-1 & -1 \\
1 & -3
\end{array}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{4}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3^{2 n+1}+1 & 3^{2 n+1}-3 \\
3^{2 n}-1 & 3^{2 n}+3
\end{array}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
$$
\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad\binom{x_{2 n+2}}{x_{2 n+1}} & =\left(\begin{array}{ll}
2 & 3 \\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right)\binom{x_{2 n+1}}{x_{2 n}} \\
& =\left(\begin{array}{ll}
2 & 3 \\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right)^{2}\binom{x_{2 n}}{x_{2 n-1}} \\
& =\left(\begin{array}{ll}
2 & 3 \\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right)^{3}\binom{x_{2 n-1}}{x_{2 n-2}}
\end{aligned}
$$
⋮
$$
\begin{aligned}
& =\left(\begin{array}{ll}
2 & 3 \\
1 & 0
\end{array}\right)^{2 n}\binom{x_{2}}{x_{1}} \\
& =\frac{1}{4}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
3^{2 n+1}+1 & 3^{2 n+1}-3 \\
3^{2 n}-1 & 3^{2 n}+3
\end{array}\right)\binom{4}{0} \\
& =\binom{3^{2 n+1}+1}{3^{2 n}-1}
\end{aligned}
$$
$$
\therefore \quad x_{2 n+2}=3^{2 n+1}+1
$$
i.e.$\quad x_{2 n}=\underline{3^{2 n-1}+1}$ 1A （6）

